window.LESSONS["d11-bleeding"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: read the product off the tracing, and separate surgical from coagulopathic bleeding",
    html: "<p>The junior transfuses a panel; the senior transfuses a <b>deficit</b>, read straight off the viscoelastic tracing (TEG/ROTEM) so each unit fixes a demonstrated defect and nothing more:</p><ul><li><b>Prolonged initiation</b> (long R-time / EXTEM CT) → clotting-factor deficit or residual heparin → <b>FFP or PCC</b>, or <b>more protamine</b> if the heparin-specific channel (heparinase/HEPTEM vs INTEM) says heparin is the culprit.</li><li><b>Weak clot / low alpha-angle / low FIBTEM amplitude</b> → hypofibrinogenemia → <b>cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate</b> (target fibrinogen roughly &gt; 1.5–2 g/L in the bleeding patient).</li><li><b>Low maximum amplitude / MCF with adequate fibrinogen</b> → platelet number or function → <b>platelets ± desmopressin</b> (the aspirin/uremic platelet).</li><li><b>Accelerated lysis</b> (high LY30 / EXTEM ML) → hyperfibrinolysis → <b>an antifibrinolytic (tranexamic acid)</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Surgical versus coagulopathic — the decision that a blood product cannot fix.</b> A normal TEG with a bleeding chest points at a <i>suture</i>, not a factor. Use rate-based re-exploration triggers: roughly <b>&gt; 400 mL/hr for 1 hour, &gt; 300 mL/hr for 2–3 hours, or &gt; 200 mL/hr for 4 hours</b> — and the inverse trap, the <b>sudden fall to near-zero output with rising filling pressures</b>, which is clot obstructing the tubes and tamponade, not a cure. For the patient who declines blood products, the plan is built <i>before</i> incision: maximize preoperative red-cell mass, cell salvage, retrograde autologous priming, meticulous antifibrinolysis, and acceptance of a lower transfusion trigger.</p><div class='kbox kbox-study'><div class='kbox-tag'>STUDY</div><p>Every product you give an actively bleeding cardiac patient should answer a specific tracing abnormality. \"Diffuse ooze plus a normal TEG\" is the classic signature that the problem is on the <i>field</i>, and the answer is the OR, not the blood bank.</p></div>"
  }
);

window.LESSONS["d11-coag"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 5,
    title: "PGY-5 mastery: the evolving bleeding chest and the catastrophic protamine reaction",
    html: "<p><b>Run the bleeding chest as a moving picture, not a snapshot.</b> Trend drainage, ACT, core temperature, fibrinogen, platelets, and the TEG <i>against</i> the hemodynamics, and at each step re-ask: is targeted correction still winning, or has delay itself become the lethal variable? Warm the patient (coagulation enzymes stall below ~34–35 °C), correct ionized calcium and pH, give the tracing-directed product — but the moment the rate crosses the re-exploration threshold with a corrected coagulopathy, or output vanishes with rising CVP, the decision converts from medical to surgical. Hesitation dressed up as \"one more round of product\" is how a coagulopathic ooze becomes an exsanguination or a tamponade arrest.</p><p><b>The catastrophic (Type III) protamine reaction</b> is the dosed algorithm to own: within minutes of protamine, catastrophic <b>pulmonary vasoconstriction → acute RV pressure overload and collapse</b>. The ordered response:</p><ol><li><b>Stop the protamine immediately.</b></li><li><b>Support the circulation</b> — 100% oxygen, epinephrine/vasopressor for systemic pressure, and <b>offload the RV</b>.</li><li><b>Treat the pulmonary hypertension</b> with an <b>inhaled pulmonary vasodilator</b> (inhaled nitric oxide or prostacyclin) and RV inotropy.</li><li>If the RV is arresting, <b>re-heparinize and return to cardiopulmonary bypass</b> to support the circulation while the reaction abates, then re-attempt reversal cautiously.</li></ol><p>Note the trap embedded in it: the reflex to give <i>more</i> protamine (assuming rebound heparin) intensifies a Type III reaction — the drug is the poison here, not the antidote.</p><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>VIVA</div><p><i>\"Chest tubes 400 mL/hr for two hours, ACT normal, core 35.2 °C, TEG shows a long R-time with a low alpha-angle — surgical or medical, what do you give and in what order, and when do you go back?\"</i> Answer: mixed — the tracing shows factor deficit + low fibrinogen (give FFP/PCC then cryo/fibrinogen, warm, correct Ca²⁺/pH), but a normal ACT with a &gt;300–400 mL/hr rate for 2 h is a re-exploration trigger — correct what you can en route to the OR, do not keep transfusing a surgical bleed.</p></div>"
  }
);
